>In article <Nov.8.05.14.32.1989.16633@topaz.rutgers.edu> linhart@topaz.rutgers.edu (D-ro 3.) writes:
>>Locked doors are closed doors.  Broken doors are displayed as no doors.
>>To illustrate:

>>closed			--+--				|
>>							|
>>open, horizontal	--|--	open, vertical		-
>>							|
>>none			--.--				|

>>Doorless doorways can be entered diagonally.

>I think these are good, except for the no_doorway. I think that should
>appear as the corridor symbol '#' rather than as the floor symbol '.'.

Well, being the person who made the doors look like this, I should be the
right person to answer this..

The answer is NO. Absolutely not, though you can (if you so want) configure
the open-door-symbols to look like corridors, but I'll tell you why not.

An example situation. The player is aproaching a room: 
(This is how your suggestion would look like: )
1)
	##@#

2)
	###@#

3)          -----
	####@...|
	    |...|
	    -----
(This is the righ way: )
1)
	##@#

2)
	###@.

3)          -----
	####@...|
	    |...|
	    -----

See the difference? When you aproach an doorway from the corridor, _you_
can't see the difference. Bye the way, I'm pretty glad someone from the team
made the addition that doorways can be entered diagonally!
(One further suggestion. When being a rock mole the player can't eat away the
door. Perhaps he should be able to do it?)
